<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narapatisithu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1150–1211</span> · Also known as Sithu II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narapatisithu, also known as Sithu II, was king of Pagan from 1174 to 1211, a reign often seen as the height of the kingdom. He restored stability after a period of violent successions, organised a palace guard, supported the reform of the monastic order by monks ordained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and built the Sulamani temple. Burmese increasingly replaced Mon as the main written language under his rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narapatisithu.jpg" alt="Narapatisithu" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jialiang Gao (peace-on-earth.org) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gawdawpalin_Temple_Bagan_Myanmar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
